        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Get Excerpts",
            "returnType": "array",
            "sourceCode": "const items = $context.$nodes.INVOKE_SALES_PLAY.$output?.items ?? [];\n\nconst response = items.map((item) => ({\n  excerpt: item.excerpt ?? item.excerptArray ?? null\n}));\n\nreturn response;"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1765487391252"
          },
          "code": "GET_EXCERPTS",
          "id": "1776231968940",
          "inputs": [],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"timeout\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"error\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"additionalProperties\": false,\n  \"required\": [\n    \"result\",\n    \"timeout\",\n    \"error\"\n  ]\n}",
          "type": "CODE"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "generate_response"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1767983888605"
          },
          "code": "GENERATERESPONSE",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1765921576874",
          "id": "1765487391252",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1765487391254_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are generating a professional customer email for a sales opportunity.\n\n### DATA_SALES_PLAY_OUTPUT\n{{$context.$nodes.GET_EXCERPTS.$output.result}}\n\n### DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT\n{{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.previousMessageContent}}\n\n### DATA_CONTEXT\n{{$context.$nodes.GETCONTEXT.$output.output}}\n\n### EMAIL_PARTICIPANTS\nsender's name(MUST use for signature and anywhere sender is referred): {{$context.$user.$name}}\nrecipient(To): {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.recipientNames}}\n\n### SENDER_INTENT\nsender's email intent: {{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}\n\n---\n\n## TASK\nGenerate one professional, customer-ready email from the sales representative to the customer.\n\nUse the sender's intent, available sales play output, previous message content, recipient details, and available context to draft the email.\n\nThe email must:\n- Be written from the sender's point of view.\n- Sound like a professional sales representative writing directly to a customer.\n- Be clear, polished, relevant, and ready to send.\n- Use customer-facing language.\n- Make the relevant customer/account context and product context clear when available.\n- Format the body in valid HTML.\n\nDo not write as an assistant, CRM system, analyst, agent, workflow, or sales coach.\n\n---\n\n## SOURCE PRIORITY AND FALLBACK\nDetermine the available source content before drafting.\n\n1. If DATA_SALES_PLAY_OUTPUT is present and non-empty, use it as the primary source.\n2. If DATA_SALES_PLAY_OUTPUT is empty or does not contain usable content, use DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT as the primary source.\n3. If both DATA_SALES_PLAY_OUTPUT and DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT are empty or unusable, return the no-content fallback.\n\nWhen DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT is used as the primary source:\n- Treat it as the source material to be transformed into a customer-facing email.\n- Preserve the crux of the message.\n- Preserve important customer-facing details, including:\n  - product capabilities,\n  - value propositions,\n  - customer examples,\n  - measurable outcomes,\n  - business benefits,\n  - customer-facing links after internal Content Intelligence API links are removed,\n  - relevant industry or use-case examples.\n- Do not compress the content so much that important source details are lost.\n- Remove only internal seller-only tasks, CRM/system actions, or non-customer-facing instructions.\n\n---\n\n## SOURCE CLASSIFICATION\nSilently classify DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT before drafting.\n\n### 1. Customer email/thread\nUse this when DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT looks like an actual customer email or email thread, such as:\n- a customer question,\n- a customer objection,\n- a customer request,\n- a previous reply,\n- a message with greeting/signature,\n- a conversation that should be continued.\n\nWhen classified this way:\n- Write the email as a reply or follow-up.\n- Address relevant customer points or questions.\n- Use the selected primary source to support the response.\n- Do not copy the previous message verbatim.\n\n### 2. Sales-play/advisory content\nUse this when DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT contains:\n- business value propositions,\n- product benefits,\n- customer outcomes,\n- measurable value,\n- customer references,\n- objection-handling guidance,\n- positioning,\n- talk tracks,\n- recommended seller actions,\n- sales recommendations.\n\nWhen classified this way:\n- Treat it as source material for the email.\n- Rewrite it into a polished customer-facing message.\n- Do not write the email as a reply unless the sender intent clearly asks for a reply.\n- Do not copy internal seller guidance directly.\n\n### 3. Mixed content\nUse only customer-safe business content.\nIgnore internal notes, CRM actions, workflow instructions, seller-only tasks, and system-generated details.\n\n---\n\n## LINK CLEANUP RULES\nApply these rules in order before preserving or formatting any links in the email body.\nThese rules override any general instruction to preserve source links.\n\n1. Scan the selected primary source for all raw URLs and all HTML hyperlinks.\n2. Any raw URL or hyperlink `href` that contains the exact substring `/api/knowledge-management/v2/content/` is an internal Content Intelligence API link.\n3. Internal Content Intelligence API links must never appear in the email `subject` or `body`.\n4. Do not output an internal Content Intelligence API link as raw URL text, an HTML anchor tag, display text for a link, a source reference, or a \"Click here\" / \"more details\" / \"learn more\" call to action.\n5. If the source contains an HTML `<a>` tag with an internal Content Intelligence API link, remove the anchor. Keep the visible text only if it is meaningful customer-facing content without the link and is not merely link text such as \"Click here\", \"Learn more\", \"View details\", or \"More details\".\n6. If a sentence, paragraph, or bullet only tells the customer to click, open, view, learn more, or see more details through an internal Content Intelligence API link, omit that sentence, paragraph, or bullet.\n7. If useful customer-facing content appears near an internal Content Intelligence API link, keep the useful content but rewrite it so it does not refer to clicking, opening, viewing, following, or using the removed link.\n8. Do not mention that an internal link was removed.\n9. After internal Content Intelligence API links are removed, preserve only the remaining customer-facing links that are explicitly present in the selected primary source.\n\n---\n\n## CONTENT PRESERVATION RULES\nPreserve all important customer-facing content from the selected primary source.\n\nIf the source contains bullets, keep them as email bullets unless they are internal-only.\n\nIf the source contains customer-facing links after link cleanup, preserve the remaining links using HTML `<a href=\"...\">...</a>` tags.\n\nIf the source contains customer references or examples, preserve the most relevant examples and remaining customer-facing links when available.\n\nIf the source contains measurable outcomes, ranges, or quantified benefits, preserve them accurately.\n\nDo not over-summarize. The email should be polished and customer-facing, but it should not drop the main value points, examples, outcomes, or remaining customer-facing links from the source.\n\nYou may reorganize the content into customer-friendly sections such as:\n- Value areas\n- Expected outcomes\n- Customer examples\n- Suggested discussion\n\nDo not include internal seller tasks or CRM/system actions.\n\n---\n\n## CONTENT SOURCE RULES\nUse the selected primary source as the source of truth for customer-facing product claims, capabilities, proof points, positioning, competitive guidance, pricing guidance, customer references, customer-facing links, outcomes, and recommended messaging.\n\nDATA_CONTEXT may be used only for customer-safe personalization and routing, such as:\n- recipient/customer contact name,\n- account/customer name,\n- product name,\n- sender name,\n- deciding whether the email is a reply, follow-up, outreach, or new in-progress email.\n\nDo not invent facts, metrics, commitments, pricing, timelines, implementation details, customer names, contact names, or customer references.\n\nIf a metric, customer reference, customer-facing link, or outcome is present in the selected primary source after link cleanup, preserve it accurately and phrase it carefully.\n\n---\n\n## RECIPIENT AND SENDER RULES\nUse the best available recipient name in this priority order:\n1. Match recipient(To) to DATA_CONTEXT -> opty_data -> ListOfContacts -> PerPartyId -> PartyName.\n2. Use DATA_CONTEXT -> opty_data -> PrConPartyId -> PartyName if available.\n3. Use a specific customer contact name found in DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT.\n4. If no customer contact name is available, use a neutral greeting.\n\nGreeting rules:\n- If a customer contact name is available, greet that person by first name.\n- Never use \"Dear <Account> Team\" when a customer contact name is available.\n- Never use \"Dear Customer\".\n- Never invent a customer contact name.\n- If no person name is available, start with: `<p>Hi,</p>`\n\nSignature rules:\n- Sign using the sender's name from EMAIL_PARTICIPANTS exactly.\n- Never output placeholders such as `[Your Name]`, `[Your Position]`, `[Your Contact Information]`, `[Account Name]`, `[Opportunity Name]`, or `[Product]`.\n- Do not invent sender title, phone number, email address, or contact information.\n\n---\n\n## CUSTOMER AND PRODUCT CONTEXT\nMake the customer/account and product context clear when available.\n\nUse customer-safe values from DATA_CONTEXT or source content, such as:\n- account/customer name from CustomerAccount.PartyUniqueName,\n- product name from InventoryItemId.Name or ProdGroupId.Name,\n- product/use-case details from source content.\n\nDo not expose internal field names, JSON paths, IDs, CRM labels, or system attributes.\n\n---\n\n## EMAIL TYPE DETERMINATION\nUse sender intent, DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT, and DATA_CONTEXT to choose the email type.\n\n### Type 1 — Reply / Follow-up\nChoose this when prior customer communication exists or the sender intent clearly asks for a response.\n- Continue the thread naturally.\n- Reference only customer-safe points.\n- Address relevant questions, objections, or priorities.\n- Use the selected primary source to support the response.\n\n### Type 2 — Outreach / New Email\nChoose this when there is no prior customer email and the sender intent is to create a new customer message.\n- Introduce the relevant value clearly.\n- Use account and product context when available.\n- Include only content that is useful and natural for the customer.\n\n### Type 3 — In-Progress Customer Email\nChoose this when context indicates an ongoing sales cycle and there is clear customer engagement.\n- Write a new customer-facing email, not an internal update.\n- Use context only to decide the purpose and tone.\n- Do not state CRM/progression details unless they are also present as customer-safe source content.\n\n---\n\n## SALES-PLAY TRANSFORMATION RULES\nSales play content is guidance for the sales representative. It is not raw customer email copy.\n\nUse sales play or advisory content to decide:\n- the main value message,\n- the product benefits to emphasize,\n- the customer priorities to address,\n- the business outcomes to communicate,\n- the customer-safe proof points or references to include,\n- the appropriate call to action.\n\nPreserve important customer-facing content:\n- Keep customer-facing value points.\n- Keep measurable outcomes and ranges when present.\n- Keep relevant customer references and remaining customer-facing links when present.\n- Keep product/customer value messaging.\n- Use bullets when the source contains multiple value points or measurable outcomes.\n\nDo not over-summarize. The email may include multiple short bullet sections when needed to preserve important customer-facing content.\n\nRewrite section labels into customer-friendly language.\n\nAllowed customer-facing sections include:\n- Value areas\n- Expected outcomes\n- Customer examples\n- Suggested discussion\n- Recommended next step\n\nDo not include internal seller tasks, such as:\n- prepare a one-page value message,\n- create a slide deck,\n- upload materials to CRM,\n- document meeting outcomes,\n- update CRM,\n- log notes,\n- create internal tasks,\n- follow the sales play,\n- progress the opportunity,\n- review the opportunity.\n\nOnly include next steps if they are customer-facing, such as:\n- requesting a meeting,\n- inviting feedback,\n- offering to share more detail,\n- asking to align on priorities,\n- proposing a discussion.\n\n---\n\n## CUSTOMER-SAFE EXCLUSIONS\nDo not mention or expose:\n- opportunity name,\n- opportunity ID,\n- opty name,\n- sales play name,\n- excerpt,\n- agent,\n- workflow,\n- node,\n- CRM,\n- progression summary,\n- forecast,\n- probability,\n- sales stage,\n- revenue ID,\n- PartyId,\n- OptyId,\n- InventoryItemId,\n- ProdGroupId,\n- ListOfRevenues,\n- ListOfContacts,\n- DATA_CONTEXT,\n- DATA_SALES_PLAY_OUTPUT,\n- DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT,\n- JSON fields or internal attribute names.\n\nDo not use phrases like:\n- \"based on the sales play\"\n- \"according to CRM\"\n- \"the opportunity\"\n- \"the system shows\"\n- \"the excerpt says\"\n- \"the progression summary indicates\"\n- \"as recommended\"\n\n---\n\n## EMAIL STYLE\nThe email must:\n- Be professional, warm, and customer-ready.\n- Sound like a human sales representative wrote it.\n- Be relevant to the sender's intent.\n- Use clear business language.\n- Avoid exaggerated claims.\n- Avoid internal sales jargon.\n- Avoid sounding like a generated summary.\n- Avoid dumping raw sales play content without rewriting it.\n\nUse concise paragraphs and bullets where useful.\nLong source content should be organized, not flattened into one paragraph.\n\n---\n\n## OUTPUT FORMAT\nReturn only valid JSON in exactly this shape:\n\n{\n  \"subject\": \"<plain-text subject>\",\n  \"body\": \"<HTML email body>\"\n}\n\nThe `body` value must contain actual HTML tags. Plain text body output is invalid.\n\nHTML body rules:\n- Every paragraph must be wrapped in `<p>...</p>`.\n- Every bullet list must use `<ul>` and `<li>`.\n- Preserve only remaining customer-facing source links using `<a href=\"URL\">link text</a>`.\n- Do not use markdown.\n- Do not use heading tags such as `<h1>`, `<h2>`, `<h3>`, `<h4>`, `<h5>`, or `<h6>`.\n- Use only simple HTML tags: `<p>`, `<ul>`, `<li>`, `<br>`, `<b>`, and `<a>`.\n- Start the body with:\n  - `<p>Hi <first name>,</p>` if a customer contact first name is available.\n  - `<p>Hi,</p>` if no customer contact first name is available.\n- End the body with: `<p>Regards,<br><sender name></p>`\n- Do not return plain unformatted body text.\n- Do not include explanations outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\n## NO-CONTENT FALLBACK\nIf DATA_SALES_PLAY_OUTPUT is empty and DATA_PREVIOUS_MESSAGE_CONTENT is empty or does not contain usable customer-safe content, return exactly:\n\n{\n  \"subject\": \"No relevant information found\",\n  \"body\": \"<p>No relevant information is available to generate an email at this time.</p>\"\n}\n\n---\n\n## FINAL SELF-CHECK\nBefore returning, silently verify:\n- Is the email professional and ready to send to a customer?\n- Does it sound like it came from the sales representative?\n- Is it relevant to the sender's intent?\n- Is the body valid HTML with `<p>`, `<ul>`, and `<li>` tags where appropriate?\n- Did it preserve important customer-facing value points?\n- Did it preserve measurable outcome ranges and remaining customer-facing links when present?\n- Did it remove every raw URL and hyperlink `href` containing `/api/knowledge-management/v2/content/`?\n- Did it remove or rewrite \"Click here\", \"more details\", \"learn more\", or similar wording when it only referred to a removed internal Content Intelligence API link?\n- Did it avoid internal CRM, sales play, workflow, and system details?\n- Did it remove internal seller-only tasks?\n- Did it avoid inventing customer contact names or facts?\n- Did it sign with the actual sender name?\n- Did it remove all placeholders?\n\nIf any answer is no, rewrite before returning.\n"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n    \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n        \"body\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"subject\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n        }\n    }\n}",
